on offers a bevy of gameplay  options that mesh and there's no one  right way to play but leaning into their  strengths and mixing it up with the  occasional oddball tactic works well  that's supported with magic which can  turn the tide of all kinds of fights as  a result you're faced with a choice in  how you protect the magic wielding Lords  and maximize their potential without  risking a loss campaigns throw even more  variables into that mix like previous  total war games you can take command of  a country and balance your time managing  economies cultures and individual  battles here things go from beautifully  intricate to elaborate masterstroke each  of the four major factions have their  own campaigns with major battles quests  and goals  unlike previous total war games where  you'd have a smattering of small  distinctions to separate each group  these races are truly distinct vampires  are reviled by the living and have a  hard time with diplomacy to survive and  remain stable they have to poison and  corrupt the land in front of them doors  and green skins can travel underground  and have constructed settlements that  only they can capture each of these  pieces works together and helps texture  the overarching narrative at first these  races push their own petty agendas but  as the Game of Thrones II threat grows  in the north you can try to band  together with the others and hold off  the impending invasion at the same time  you'll have proximal race dependent  goals for victory which strain how  you'll manage these larger threats as  these campaigns progress you'll have to  manage multiple conflicts on many fronts  putting your skills to the test in a  variety of ways total war Warhammer is  an interlocking network of very smart  decisions and integrating the Warhammer  universe with total wars systems was the  first massive battles are more  challenging because of the addition of  magic and flying units a new brands of  artillery and soldiers keep you 